const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the main difference between solvent-based and waterborne coatings in film formation?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Solvent-based coatings form a film by the evaporation of organic solvent from the applied wet film, leaving behind a continuous polymer binder network. The polymer is fully dissolved in the solvent at the point of application — film formation is essentially a phase inversion from solution to solid as the solvent departs. Waterborne coatings form a film by the coalescence of polymer particles suspended in water: as the water evaporates, the latex or emulsion particles are drawn together and deform above the minimum film formation temperature (MFFT), fusing into a continuous film. This distinction has significant practical consequences — solvent-borne films are generally insensitive to application temperature (as long as solvent evaporation can proceed), while waterborne films require application and substrate temperatures above the MFFT to form a defect-free coating." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why are high-solids coatings sometimes preferred over waterborne for industrial corrosion protection?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "High-solids coatings achieve significantly higher dry film thickness per coat than conventional solvent-borne coatings — because they deposit more polymer per unit volume of applied wet film — reducing the number of coats required to meet a specified dry film thickness specification. This is particularly valuable in heavy industrial applications where applying multiple coats to complex structures (tanks, vessels, pipework) is labour-intensive and expensive. High-solids formulations also retain the substrate wetting, application robustness in extreme temperature and humidity conditions, and chemical and corrosion resistance mechanisms of solvent-borne chemistry — advantages that are harder to replicate in waterborne systems for aggressive environments. Waterborne coatings may require more controlled application conditions and longer cure times that are not practical in all industrial maintenance painting situations." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do VOC regulations affect the choice between solvent-based and waterborne formulations?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "VOC regulations set legal ceilings on the volatile organic compound content of coating and adhesive products, expressed in grams per litre (g/L) of product ready for application. In the EU, Directive 2004/42/EC establishes VOC limit values by product category and subcategory. In the US, EPA National Emission Standards for Hazardous Air Pollutants (NESHAP) and state-level rules govern industrial coating VOC. Conventional solvent-borne coatings typically contain 300–600 g/L VOC — exceeding limits for many product categories. High-solids formulations typically achieve 150–300 g/L. Waterborne formulations typically achieve 30–150 g/L for industrial grades. Regulatory trend is consistently toward lower limits over successive review cycles, meaning high-solids systems that are compliant today may require further reformulation in future. Manufacturers operating in multiple jurisdictions must assess VOC compliance requirements for each market independently, as limits vary substantially between regions." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can waterborne coatings match the performance of solvent-based in heavy industrial corrosion protection?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "For the majority of industrial corrosion protection applications — including atmospheric exposure categories C1 through C4 per ISO 12944-2 — modern 2K waterborne epoxy and polyurethane systems can match the performance of their solvent-borne equivalents in terms of adhesion, barrier properties, and corrosion resistance. Performance parity for C5 (very high corrosion, offshore and industrial immersion) environments remains more challenging and continues to depend on the specific system, application conditions, and substrate preparation quality. The historical performance gap between waterborne and solvent-borne industrial coatings has closed substantially since the early 2000s through improvements in dispersion resin technology, coalescent and co-solvent optimisation, and crosslinker development. The key discriminator for most applications is not performance ceiling but application robustness — solvent-borne systems tolerate a wider range of application conditions (temperature, humidity) and contaminated substrates." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is minimum film formation temperature (MFFT) and why does it matter?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Minimum film formation temperature (MFFT) is the lowest temperature at which a waterborne coating can form a continuous, defect-free film as the water phase evaporates. Below the MFFT, the latex polymer particles are too rigid to deform and coalesce — the dried film remains powdery, cracked, or discontinuous, with severely compromised barrier properties, adhesion, and appearance. MFFT is a fundamental physical property of the waterborne binder system and is determined primarily by the glass transition temperature (Tg) of the polymer. Formulators lower MFFT toward the required application temperature range through the use of coalescent solvents (added temporarily to plasticise the particles during film formation and then evaporate from the cured film) or by selecting polymer systems with inherently low Tg. MFFT is critically important for construction and maintenance painting in cold or variable ambient conditions." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is UV-cure always the best option for achieving near-zero VOC in industrial coatings?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "UV-cure (and electron beam cure) systems are technically the most efficient approach to near-zero VOC — they are formulated as 100% reactive systems with essentially no solvent content. However, UV cure requires line-of-sight exposure to UV radiation, which limits its application to flat or regular substrates (sheets, panels, web-fed materials) and excludes three-dimensional assembled parts, large structures, and maintenance painting. The capital cost of UV curing equipment is substantial, and the photoinitiator chemistry adds formulation cost. UV-cure is therefore the optimal choice for high-volume flat substrate applications — wood panels, printed circuit boards, optical films, flexible packaging — but is not a general-purpose replacement for solvent-borne or waterborne systems in construction, maintenance, automotive assembly, and structural applications where three-dimensional access or large-area application is required." }
    }
